Unit: AHCLPW313 - Undertake Sampling and Testing of Water
Course: Certificate III in Conservation and Ecosystem Management
Delivery Mode: Classroom and Field-Based Learning
Total Nominal Hours: 40
Sessions: 10 (4 hours each)
________________________________________
Session Plan Overview
Each session will cover specific elements and performance criteria of the unit. Sessions will include theory, practical activities, and self-directed learning tasks.
________________________________________
Session 1: Introduction to Water Sampling and Testing
Learning Outcomes:
•	Understand the importance of water quality testing
•	Identify key legislative and workplace requirements
•	Recognise different water sampling techniques
Activities:
•	Group discussion: Why is water quality important?
•	Presentation on legislation (Water Act, Environmental Protection policies)
•	Video: Overview of water testing techniques
•	Self-directed learning: Research local water bodies and their uses
Teacher Prompts:
•	"What are some key factors affecting water quality in our region?"
•	"Why do different industries have different water quality standards?"
Useful Links:
•	National Water Quality Management Strategy
•	Tasmanian Environmental Protection Authority
________________________________________
Session 2: Planning for Water Sampling
Learning Outcomes:
•	Develop a water sampling plan
•	Identify safe work procedures and risk assessments
Activities:
•	Case study: Designing a water sampling plan for a local creek
•	Hazard identification and risk assessment workshop
•	Equipment overview: pH meters, turbidity tubes, dissolved oxygen sensors
•	Self-directed learning: Read Safe Work Australia guidelines on water sampling
Teacher Prompts:
•	"What risks might be present when sampling in different environments?"
•	"How can you ensure consistency in sampling techniques?"
Useful Links:
•	Safe Work Australia - Environmental Sampling
________________________________________
Session 3: Field Techniques for Water Sampling
Learning Outcomes:
•	Demonstrate correct water sampling techniques
•	Follow chain of custody procedures
Activities:
•	Field trip: Collecting water samples from different sources
•	Hands-on: Correct use of sampling containers and preservation methods
•	Self-directed learning: Document field observations in a report
Teacher Prompts:
•	"Why is it important to label and preserve samples correctly?"
•	"What factors can influence sample contamination?"
________________________________________
Session 4: On-Site Water Testing Methods
Learning Outcomes:
•	Conduct on-site testing for pH, temperature, and dissolved oxygen
Activities:
•	Practical: Use of handheld meters and field kits
•	Small group discussion: Interpreting real-time water quality results
•	Self-directed learning: Research common water pollutants and their effects
Teacher Prompts:
•	"How do environmental conditions impact on-site test results?"
•	"What limitations do field tests have compared to lab tests?"
________________________________________
Session 5: Laboratory Testing and Data Interpretation
Learning Outcomes:
•	Understand laboratory analysis techniques
•	Interpret water quality data
Activities:
•	Guest speaker: Water lab technician
•	Data analysis workshop: Comparing lab and field results
•	Self-directed learning: Write a reflection on lab testing processes
Teacher Prompts:
•	"What are the advantages of lab testing over field testing?"
•	"How can data trends inform environmental management decisions?"
________________________________________
Session 6: Assessing and Reporting Water Quality
Learning Outcomes:
•	Compare test results to water quality guidelines
•	Document findings in a report
Activities:
•	Group activity: Present findings from field and lab tests
•	Report writing exercise using a structured template
•	Self-directed learning: Review a government water quality report
Teacher Prompts:
•	"What challenges arise when interpreting water quality data?"
•	"How can reporting influence environmental decision-making?"
________________________________________
Session 7: Water Quality Management Strategies
Learning Outcomes:
•	Identify water quality management strategies
•	Understand remediation methods
Activities:
•	Case study: Water pollution event and response strategies
•	Brainstorm session: Strategies for improving water quality
•	Self-directed learning: Research best practices for catchment management
Teacher Prompts:
•	"What are some common causes of water contamination?"
•	"How can land management practices improve water quality?"
________________________________________
Session 8: Advanced Water Testing Techniques
Learning Outcomes:
•	Conduct advanced testing methods (turbidity, salinity, EC, BOD)
Activities:
•	Hands-on: Use of advanced sensors for data collection
•	Data comparison exercise: Field vs. lab results
•	Self-directed learning: Research the role of macroinvertebrates in water quality assessment
Teacher Prompts:
•	"How do different pollutants impact water ecosystems?"
•	"What role does biodiversity play in water health monitoring?"
________________________________________
Session 9: Final Field Assessment
Learning Outcomes:
•	Demonstrate proficiency in field sampling and testing
•	Apply risk management strategies
Activities:
•	Independent field assessment: Conduct full water sampling procedure
•	Peer review: Feedback on techniques
•	Self-directed learning: Prepare a summary report of findings
Teacher Prompts:
•	"What challenges did you encounter in the field?"
•	"How would you improve your sampling approach next time?"
________________________________________
Session 10: Review and Assessment Submission
Learning Outcomes:
•	Finalise and submit reports
•	Reflect on learning outcomes
Activities:
•	Peer presentations on water quality reports
•	Final assessment submission
•	Group discussion: Career pathways related to water quality management
Teacher Prompts:
•	"How has your understanding of water sampling changed?"
•	"What are some key takeaways from this unit?"
________________________________________
Assessment Overview
•	Practical Demonstration (Field and Lab Sampling) – 40%
•	Report Writing (Data Interpretation & Comparison) – 30%
•	Knowledge Test (Legislation & Methods) – 20%
•	Self-Reflection Assignment – 10%
________________________________________
Additional Resources:
•	Australian Water Quality Guidelines
•	Waterwatch Australia
•	CSIRO Water Research
This plan ensures students gain hands-on experience while developing a strong theoretical foundation in water sampling and testing.

